  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 236 PM MDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nLatest operational and ensemble model data remain in good agreement through \nthe end of the work week, with transitory upper ridging building across \neastern Colorado through Thursday, as southwest flow increases across western \nColorado ahead of an eastern Pacific storm system digging into the Great Basin. \nModel data supports abundant sup-tropical moisture becoming entrained within\nthe southwest flow aloft, with PWATS of 0.8 to over 1 inch (200-300 percent of\nnormal) moving across the region in the Wednesday and Thursday timeframe.  \nThis, along with increasing orographic flow, and uvv associated with the \nPacific system moving across the Great Basin, will lead to increasing coverage\nand intensity of precipitation along and west of the Continental Divide \nWednesday, with the heaviest precipitation expected Thursday into Thursday\nnight, as the main system starts to lift out across the Great Basin into \nthe Northern Rockies. \n\nLatest model data continues to support qpf of 1 to 3 inches along and west \nof the ContDvd through this period, with probabilities of >3 inches of liquid \nqpf in the 60 to 80 percent range across the San Juan Mtns. The Pacific origin\nof the system is expected to keep snow levels around peak level (at and above \n12,000 feet) through the duration of the heavy precipitation event (at least \nthrough Thursday night), leading to concerns of river and stream flooding as \nwell as the potential for rock/mudslides across the higher terrain of the \nContDvd. However, the area of greatest concern remains across the San Juan \nMtns, with already saturated soils from previous precipitation last week, as \nwell the potential for rain on top of snowpack already in place, with SNOTEL\ndata indicating SWE 3/4 to 1\" across the San Juans at this time. Stay tuned \nfor more information on this potentially high impact event unfolding later \nthis week, with Flood Watches likely being issued as we get closer in time \nto this heavy precipitation event.    \n    \nFurther east, with the transitory ridge, warm and mainly dry conditions will\nbe in place, with chances of showers spreading east across the plains in \nlater Thursday, as the Pacific system lifts out across the Northern Rockies. \nWhile models differ on location and strength of the system lifting out through\nthe weekend, most solutions do indicate broad troughing translating across\nthe region, bringing cooler, closer to seasonal temperatures, and unsettled \nweather to the area through next weekend.",
